/E (A while loop is most natural because blank input must not consume an attempt: increment the counter only after a non-blank value. In a for range\(3\) loop, continue still consumes an iteration, so it cannot directly satisfy the blank-entry rule without a separate counter-controlled loop. break exits on success; continue skips a blank; pass is a no-operation placeholder and is not needed in finished logic. The loop else runs only if no break occurs.)
