633 0 obj
<<
/Title (The supplied samples normalise to TN09AB1234 \(valid\), KA01M0007 \(valid\), TN9AB1234 \(invalid\) and TN221234AB \(invalid\). Receipt formatting groups the validated slices.)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 601 0 R
>>
endobj
634 0 obj
<<
/Title (The benchmark measures 1,000, 10,000 and 200,000 lines using time.perf_counter\(\). Run it three times and report the mean from your own machine; timings vary by hardware, Python build and background load. The program verifies both methods produce identical text.)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 603 0 R
>>
endobj
635 0 obj
<<
/Title (Writing line-by-line to a file avoids holding the entire statement in memory; io.StringIO provides a file-like in-memory buffer. join\(\) takes O\(total characters\) time and O\(total output\) memory. Repeated concatenation can approach O\(total characters squared\) copying in an unfavourable implementation. File streaming uses O\(one line\) working memory.)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 604 0 R
>>
endobj
636 0 obj
<<
/Title (Local names exist inside a function. global allows rebinding a module-level name; nonlocal allows rebinding a name in an enclosing function. Mutating a referenced list does not require global, but rebinding a global name does. Prefer returning updated values rather than changing global state.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 606 0 R
>>
endobj
637 0 obj
<<
/Title (re.fullmatch\(r'[A-Z]{2}[0-9]{2}[A-Z]{1,2}[0-9]{4}', reg\) expresses the grammar compactly. Both methods are O\(L\) in input length; slicing is transparent for this fixed format, while a regular expression is easier to extend for more complex patterns.)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 632 0 R
>>
endobj
